In Python, static methods and class methods are used when a method does not need access to instance data (self).
🔹 1️ Static Method
What is a Static Method?
•	Belongs to the class
•	Does NOT use self or cls
•	Works like a normal function, but inside a class
•	Defined using @staticmethod
Syntax
class MyClass:
    @staticmethod
    def static_method():
        print("This is a static method")
Usage
MyClass.static_method()
Example (Utility Function)
class MathUtils:
    @staticmethod
    def add(a, b):
        return a + b
print(MathUtils.add(5, 3))
📌 Output:
8



🔹 2️ Class Method
What is a Class Method?
•	Uses class reference (cls)
•	Can access & modify class variables
•	Defined using @classmethod
Syntax
class MyClass:
    @classmethod
    def class_method(cls):
        print("This is a class method")
Example (Working with Class Variables)
class Employee:
    company = "TCS"   # class variable

    def __init__(self, name):
        self.name = name

    @classmethod
    def change_company(cls, new_company):
        cls.company = new_company
e1 = Employee("Anirudha")
Employee.change_company("Infosys")
print(e1.company)
📌 Output:
Infosys
🔹 Difference Between Static Method & Class Method
Feature	Static Method	Class Method
Decorator	@staticmethod	@classmethod
First Parameter	None	cls
Access class variables	❌ No	✅ Yes
Access instance variables	❌ No	❌ No
Use case	Utility/helper	Modify class data

🔹 When to Use What?
✔ Use static method when:
•	Logic is related to class
•	No need for class or instance data
✔ Use class method when:
•	Need to access or modify class variables
•	Alternative constructor needed
🔹 Class Method as Alternative Constructor
class Student:
    def __init__(self, name, marks):
        self.name = name
        self.marks = marks

    @classmethod
    def from_string(cls, data):
        name, marks = data.split(",")
        return cls(name, int(marks))
s = Student.from_string("Rahul,85")
print(s.name, s.marks)

